{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A decentralized network of small services is more resilient to correlated failure than a redundant monolith because it replaces systemic technical homogeneity with 'Cognitive and Substrate Diversity'.","counterconditions":["The network converges on a 'Shadow Monolith' where all independent operators use the same managed cloud provider or Kubernetes distribution (Infrastructure Mimicry).","Market pressures force a 'Best-of-Breed' homogeneity where all services adopt the same critical dependencies (e.g., a single ubiquitous logging library) creating a shared vulnerability."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"While a monolith's redundancy is typically additive (more of the same stack), a decentralized network introduces variance in operator psychology, implementation details, and infrastructure choices. This prevents 'singular brilliant mistakes'—where a single high-privilege configuration error or a specific software bug triggers a global outage—from propagating across the entire ecosystem.","optional_refs":["8787428e900b75db22f323aaf8ffc067962ba889cd23100e921e90a89b975f10"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["sha256:a350122532e72a3f38f549bd302d6cf538cd141f5bba359bf3335833c05840a2","sha256:e8df943b67cacca4ab97b6c6bad670ce5fd9a83be45b5d9771979bb26fb15f03"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.9},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The large service with internal redundancy is more resilient because decentralized networks are susceptible to 'Semantic Correlation' via shared industry standards.","counterconditions":["The monolith's coordination logic (e.g., Paxos/Raft) reaches a 'Correlation Horizon' where the complexity of maintaining consistency becomes the primary source of correlated failure."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Decentralized services often adhere to the same RFCs and API standards. A 'poison pill' request—a malformed packet that triggers an edge-case bug in a standard's interpretation—can cause simultaneous crashes across diverse implementations that all believe they are following the same rule, whereas a monolith can implement a single, robust guardrail globally.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["sha256:591747f1afbdfcded38341aec593d85c691727b7190e73bdfa6b9de96f6b64fc","sha256:6e3aea2f37a586017cc32be7ee6904145a5255d2bef25c3771ea674b5809cbcf"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.6},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"True independence of failure is distinguished from pseudo-uncorrelated failure by the presence of 'Temporal Burstiness' and 'Cross-Substrate Invariance'.","counterconditions":["The shared trigger occurs at a frequency that mimics a Poisson distribution, making the burstiness statistically invisible over the observation window."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Independent failures follow a Poisson distribution (randomly spaced over time). Pseudo-uncorrelated failures exhibit clustering; they appear independent until an external trigger (e.g., a leap second or a global DNS update) causes a spike in failures across seemingly unrelated services. If failures persist across different cloud providers (substrates) during the same window, a shared semantic or external trigger is the cause.","optional_refs":["9763afc76fe29f1a54bf616c8ed194407fc03a392d42da63fb4c354a9b86d257","89199f725d45bbaf5723e4a037844c7d03620966a6ae1a5af11d02d9b1df4fbf"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["sha256:32236a7349803107bb3ffc6b0d13cf114a14f9077112029afe2300470d80ee6c","sha256:a350122532e72a3f38f549bd302d6cf538cd141f5bba359bf3335833c05840a2"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.8},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Neither architecture is inherently more resilient; resilience is a function of the 'Diversity Index' of the software bill of materials (SBOM) rather than organizational structure.","counterconditions":["The operational overhead of managing a high-diversity stack introduces human-error correlations that exceed the technical risks of homogeneity."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The distinction between 'one large service' and 'many small services' is a red herring. The actual driver of correlated failure is the overlap in the underlying dependency graph. A monolith with diverse internal stacks (polyglot persistence/services) is more resilient than a decentralized network using a single unified framework.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["sha256:ac1169c9748f400739bf3debc78aa3f0286b1c48baff363498aed6f84a103195","sha256:26945d1c0e853a6d07bfc6f869d081def1339e615436525853ed53e448cab308"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.4}],"context_request":{"desired_retrieval_channels":[],"requested_refs":["sha256:a350122532e72a3f38f549bd302d6cf538cd141f5bba359bf3335833c05840a2","sha256:e8df943b67cacca4ab97b6c6bad670ce5fd9a83be45b5d9771979bb26fb15f03","sha256:591747f1afbdfcded38341aec593d85c691727b7190e73bdfa6b9de96f6b64fc","sha256:6e3aea2f37a586017cc32be7ee6904145a5255d2bef25c3771ea674b5809cbcf","sha256:32236a7349803107bb3ffc6b0d13cf114a14f9077112029afe2300470d80ee6c","sha256:ac1169c9748f400739bf3debc78aa3f0286b1c48baff363498aed6f84a103195","sha256:26945d1c0e853a6d07bfc6f869d081def1339e615436525853ed53e448cab308"]},"scratch_proposal":{"cluster_suggestions":[],"links":[],"new_blocks":[{"body":{"content":"Considering the 'Cascading Dependency' failure: In a decentralized network, if small services depend on a few common 'utility' services (e.g., Auth0, Cloudflare), the decentralization is an illusion. The resilience is actually bounded by the most central node in the dependency graph. This suggests a 'Centrality Metric' is needed to evaluate the claim.","why_keep_this":"Adds a network-topology dimension to the resilience argument."},"local_key":"NEW_001"}],"revisions":[],"unresolved_questions":[{"question":"Can we mathematically define the 'Correlation Horizon' where adding redundancy to a monolith increases the probability of a total system crash?","related_refs":["SCR_005"]}]},"s